window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2025-09-16"]={"AD-032-25":"Resolution No. AD-032-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -BEAUTIFICATION FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Falls Bridge Commission continues to make voluntary in lieu of tax payments to Niagara County on an annual basis, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Falls Bridge Commission continues to support the use of these funds for various activities of the Niagara County Center for Economic Development related to economic and tourism development, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is hosting the New York State Association of Counties (NYSAC) statewide fall conference and President's Reception, which will bring hundreds of elected and appointed county officials from all over New York State to Niagara Falls and Niagara County, creating a positive economic impact, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in the Niagara Falls Bridge Commission Committed Fund Balance, and\nWHEREAS, funds have been graciously received from sponsors of the event, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Falls Convention Center is uniquely situated to provide as host and culinary professionals for the end of conference event for the hundreds of elected and appointed county officials from all over New York State, and\nWHEREAS, under Niagara County's Purchasing Guidelines, the Niagara County Legislature and the requisite committee( s) have the discretion to waive the guidelines for a professional services contract, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review for approval as to legal form, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature approves the waiver of the Niagara County Purchasing Policy for Professional Services for the NYSCA conference event, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the agreement with the Niagara Falls Convention Center shall be executed pursuant to Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County allocate up to $55,000 in Niagara Falls Bridge Commission funding and sponsor donations for the 2025 NYSAC Fall Conference, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.811 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance Committed Funds  $50,000\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.811 42705.00  Gifts and Donations Revenue  $ 5,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.811 74550.29  Programs Beautification Program  $55,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-033-25":"Resolution No. AD-033-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nPURCHASE OF IN REM PROPERTY\nWHEREAS, online bidding took place from July 23, 2025 commencing at noon through August 6, 2025, at 10:00 am for the public auction for the sale of properties acquired by the County of Niagara by its In Rem Action, and\nWHEREAS, as a result of said auction, the attached list of properties were struck down to each of the named individuals for the price indicated for each parcel, said price being the highest bid price received, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that the purchase offers set forth in the attached list and any alternate bidder from the sale herein referenced be, and the same hereby are, accepted and the Chairman of the legislature is hereby authorized and directed to execute suitable quit-claim deeds, subject to the approval of the County Attorney, upon the receipt by the County Treasurer of the entire balance due on the purchase price of said properties, at which time said deeds, as provided in the terms of sale shall be recorded in the Niagara County Clerk's Office in the name of the respective purchaser.\nLot#  Serial#  SBL#  Purchaser  Bid Price\n4  2022-020  70.02-1-26  Franklin LaPort  $ 32,800.00\n5  2021-023  71.01-1-9  LPC North31 Investments LLC  $ 29,500.00\n9  2022-031  146.13-2-57  Susan Zummo  $ 39,600.00\n11  2022-183  118.20-2-1  Kohinoor Akther  $102,300.00\n13  2022-067  40.00-1-60.122  AIAmir AIFatihah  $ 1,025.00\n15  2021-071  39.00-1-39.1  Nicholas Curtis  $ 23,800.00\n16  2021-073  39.17-1-62  Gold Key Prime LLC  $ 38,000.00\n17  2021-075  39.17-1-69  Michael Bellagamba  $ 4,150.00\n18  2022-078  130.83-2-20  Sneha Pablani  $ 31,500.00\n19  2022-086  132.13-1-14  Brian Scholtisek  $ 2,900.00\n20  2022-094  144.27-2-18  General Profesional Services LLC  $ 64,400.00\n22  2022-098  145.10.3-8  Munawar Din  $  120.00\n23  2022-099  146.13-2-21  Yash Paghadal  $  1,525.00\n24  2022-100  146.13-2-3  Kenneth Candella  $  1,325.00\n25  2022-101  146.13-2-51  Donald Hardy Jr  $  2,600.00\n26  2022-102  146.13-2-52  Michael Johnson  $  2,050.00\n27  2022-103  146.13-2-53  Khalid Othman  $  2,300.00\n28  2022-104  146.13-2-54  Khalid Othman  $  2,500.00\n29  2022-105  146.13-2-55  Khalid Othman  $  3,150.00\n30  2022-106  146.13-2-56  LPC North31 Investments LLC  $  2,275.00\n31  2022-107  146.13-2-57  Mark Mundier Jr  $  3,550.00\n32  2022-108  146.61-1-13  Yash Paghadal  $  2,025.00\n33  2022-109  146.17-4-24  Randall Brown  $  1,550.00\n36  2022-138  86.17-1-67  Yogeshrie Sewdarsan  $ 13,200.00\n39  2022-171  162.12-1-5.111  Adefowope Odueyungbo  $  2,650.00\n41  2022-179  49.00-1-71.2  Nathan Cudzilo  $ 23,800.00\n43  2021-168  22.40-2-53  Kristine Washbon  $ 26,100.00\n$ 460,695.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-034-25":"Resolution No. AD-034-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nABOLISH REAL PROPERTY TAX SERVICES AIDE POSITION/CREATE & FILL ACCOUNT\nCLERICAL III (HELP PROGRAM) - REAL PROPERTY\nWHEREAS, the duties of Real Property Tax Services Aide do not meet the present needs of the Real Property Department, and\nWHEREAS, the duties of Account Clerical III (HELP Program) represent the duties necessary for the daily operations of the Real Property Department, and\nWHEREAS, the flexibility and scope of duties of Account Clerical III (HELP Program) better serves the Real Property Department's needs, and\nWHEREAS, the change reflects no cost increase to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective September 16, 2025 the position of Real Property Tax Services Aide (Position #10524), CSEA Job Group Vat $42,042 to $46,901 per year, be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective September 16, 2025 the position of Account Clerical III (HELP Program), CSEA Job Group VI at $43,589 to $49,413 per year, be created and filled.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-035-25":"Resolution No. AD-035-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nABOLISH REAL PROPERTY INFORMATION CLERK POSITION/CREATE & FILL TAX MAP\nTECHNICIAN (HELP PROGRAM)-REAL PROPERTY\nWHEREAS, the duties of Information Clerk do not meet the present needs of the Real Property Department, and\nWHEREAS, the duties of Tax Map Technician (Help Program) represent the duties necessary for the daily operations of the Real Property Department, and\nWHEREAS, the flexibility and scope of duties of Tax Map Technician better serves the Real Property Depmtment's needs, and\nWHEREAS, the change reflects no cost increase to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective September 16, 2025 the position of Real Property Information Clerk (Position #111), CSEA Job Group VII at $45,700 to $52,507 per year, be abolished, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that effective September 16, 2005 the position of Tax Map Technician, CSEA Job Group VI at $43,589 to $49,413 per year, be created and filled.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","AD-036-25":"Resolution No. AD-036-25\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nCONTRACT WITH A VENU INSIGHTS & ANALYTICS, LLC\nSHORT TERM RENTAL COMPLIANCE SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the short term rental business has grown exponentially over recent years, which led New York State to pass recent legislation for the regulation of short term rentals, and\nWHEREAS, this new legislation provides local governments such as Niagara County with guidance on regulating this expanding industry, and\nWHEREAS in response, Niagara County adopted Local Law No. 1 for 2025, imposing a tax on the occupancy of hotel rooms pursuant to tax law subsection 1202-t, and\nWHEREAS to comply with the local law, Niagara County issued a Request for Qualifications for professional services to assist the Niagara County Treasurer's Office with short term rental compliance services, and\nWHEREAS, the awarded company shall assist the County in a number of things, including but not limited to: identifying property listings and owners, identifying non-compliance, provide monitoring services, including compliance with zoning and permitting, perform community outreach, host an online registration page and to meet any requirements imposed upon Niagara County by any New York State stuatute rules or regulations concerning short term rentals, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has selected a competitive bid for professional services as a result of the RFP process, and would like to grant the contract to Avenu Insights & Analytics, LLC for the time period of one year with four-one year extensions upon approval , and\nWHEREAS, the fee for services for the first year would be $29,916 with a 5% increase each year should the County elect to extend the contract, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the contract, the County Attorney will review the contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nApproved for Submission.\nMoved by Godfrey, seconded by Nemi, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Godfrey, seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-039-25":"Resolution No. CS-039-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - UTILITY CHARGES STEVENS STREET\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Health is required to pay new Stevens Street location utility expenses, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Department of Health will reimburse 36% state aid for article 6 program utility expenses beginning with occupation expected in August 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the County will cover the balance from the Low-Cost Power Committed Fund Balance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.20.4010.000 40599.01  Low-Cost Power Committed Fund Balance  $ 26,389.55\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.20.4090.000 43401.00  State Aid  $ 4,310.99\nA.20.4189.401 43401.00  State Aid  $  1,332.01\nA.20.4010.000 43401.00  State Aid  $  1,630.95\nCM.20.4046.418 43401.00  State Aid  $  102.43\nCM.20.4046.418 45031.02  Interfund Transfers from Committed FB  $  182.11\nCM.20.4189.406 43401.00  State Aid  $  1,334.09\nCM.20.4189.406 45031.02  Interfund Transfers from Committed FB  $ 2,371.73\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.07.9901.000 79010.00  Contribution to Other Funds  $ 2,553.84\nA.20.2960.000 74850.02  Electric  $  624.68\nA.20.2960.000 74850.03  Gas  $  624.68\nA.20.4059.000 74850.02  Electric  $ 2,092.23\nA.20.4059.000 74850.03  Gas  $ 2,092.23\nA.20.4090.000 74850.02  Electric  $ 6,172.66\nA.20.4090.000 74850.03  Gas  $ 6,172.66\nA.20.4189.401 74850.02  Electric  $ 1,850.05\nA.20.4189.401 74850.03  Gas  $ 1,850.05\nA.20.4010.000 74850.02  Electric  $ 6,265.21\nA.20 4010.000 74850.03  Gas  $ 2,365.21\nCM.20.4189.406 74850.02  Electric  $ 1,852.91\nCM.20.4189.406 74850.03  Gas  $ 1,852.91\nCM.20.4046.418 74850.02  Electric  $  142.27\nCM.20.4046.418 74850.03  Electric  $  142.27\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-040-25":"Resolution No. CS-040-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION\nFLEXIBLE FUND FOR FAMILY SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the Executive Budget did make Federal funds available to local districts to provide a comprehensive array of services to meet the needs of eligible Temporary Assistance for Needy Families (T ANF) and individuals, and\nWHEREAS, these funds enable Niagara County to direct resources toward a number of areas of program needs, including, but not limited to Employment related activities, Child Welfare Services, PINS Detention Diversion Services, Substance Abuse Assessments, Domestic Violence Screenings, Title XX Services, JD/PINS Fostercare Costs, Child Care & Development, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Dept. of Social Services will continue to contract with various agencies that will provide the necessary services in accordance with the Niagara County Flexible Fund for Family Services Plan submitted to NYS Office of Temporary and Disability Assistance (OTDA) and NYS Office of Children and Family Services (OCFS) at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Social Services 2025 budget: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.22.6010.000 44610.00  DSS Admin Revenue  $ 125,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.22.6010.000 74500.01  Contractual Exp-Contractual $125,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-041-25":"Resolution No. CS-041-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY YOUTH BUREAU - NEW YORK STATE AID APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has been designated as eligible to receive State Aid for 2025-26 youth programs through its Youth Bureau from the New York State Office of Children and Family Services in the amount of$251,016, $130,191, $104,357, $122,200, and\nWHEREAS, the Comprehensive Planning Law mandates the Niagara County Youth Bureau must apply for such funds, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review the documents for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of the County Youth Bureau be, and hereby is, authorized to make application for the following program funds:\nYouth Development Program  $251,016\nYouth Sports and Education Opportunity  $130,191\nYouth Team Sports  $104,357\nRunaway and Homeless Youth  $122,200\nTOTAL  $605,764\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute any and all documents relating to the application, subject to the review by the County Manager and the approval of the County Attorney.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-042-25":"Resolution No. CS-042-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\n2025 CONTRACT STATE AID COLA/MINIMUM WAGE-MENTAL HEALTH DEPARTMENT\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Mental Health (OMH) and New York State Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS) has approved additional State Aid COLA/Minimum Wage for the calendar year of 2025 for various agencies, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of Community Services is hereby authorized to engage in the process to carry out the necessary agreements between the County and the various sub-contractors as aforesaid, subject to the approval of the County Attorney's Office, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $  783\nINCREASE REVENUES:\nA.21.4322.412.43490.09  Mental Health Association  $  1,648\nA.21.4322.413.43490.12  WNY Independent Living  $  4,489\nA.21.4322.414.43490. IO  Northpointe Council  $ 63,878\nA.21.4322.424.43490. l 0  Cazenovia Recovery  $ 43,926\nA.2 l .4322.425.43490. l 0  BestSelf Behavioral Health  $  6,860\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.0l  Mental Health Programs  $206,196\n(Community Missions (CMI), Dale Association,\nMental Health Association (MHA), United Cerebral Palsy, Spectrum)\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.05  Reinvestment Programming (CMI, MHA, New Directions)  $ 17,492\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.08  Community Support Services (Community Missions, MHA)  $  8,480\nCM.21.4322.416.43489.04  Case Management Programs (Community Missions)  $  3,275\nCM.21.4322.423.43490.14  Supported Housing (Community Missions, DePaul)  $ 48,941\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4322.412.74500.0l  Mental Health Association  $  2,431\nA.21.4322.413.74500.01  WNY Independent Living  $  4,489\nA.21.4322.414.74550.08  Northpointe Council  $ 63,878\nA.2 l .4322.424.74500.08  Cazenovia Recovery  $ 43,926\nA.21.4322.425.74500.0l  BestSelfBehavioral Health  $  6,860\nCM.21.4322.415.74500.0l  Community Support/MH Services Programs  $214,676\n(Community Missions, Dale Association, Mental Health Association,\nUnited Cerebral Palsy, Spectrum)\nCM.21.4322.415.74550.06  Reinvestment Programming (CMI, MHA, New Directions)  $  17,492\nCM.21.4322.416.74500.0l  Case Management Programs (Community Missions, MHA)  $  3,275\nCM.21.4322.423.74500.01  Supported Housing (Community Missions, DePaul)  $ 48,941\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-043-25":"Resolution No. CS-043-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF MENTAL HEALTH & SUBSTANCE ABUSE SVCS -\nABOLISH FULL TIME SENIOR LICENSED CLINICIAN POSITION AND PART TIME LICENSED\nCLINICIAN/ CREATE AND FILL FULL TIME SUPERVISING LICENSED CLINICIAN\nBUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health & Substance Abuse Services (NCDMH) is committed to providing exceptional treatment and integrated care services to Niagara County residents in need, and\nWHEREAS, position changes within the Department is necessary to address departmental and client needs, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH has evaluated the work and responsibilities within the Crisis Services program, and has determined that the nature of the responsibilities in oversight of the work of the Crisis Counselors and Mobile Crisis Team requires a supervisor (verses senior) who also has the ability to provide mental health diagnosis, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health & Substance Abuse Services (NCDMH) is committed to providing exceptional treatment and integrated care services to Niagara County residents in need, and\nWHEREAS, position changes within the Department is necessary to address departmental and client needs, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH has evaluated the work and responsibilities within the Assisted Outpatient Treatment (AOT) program and has determined the work has proven to be more case management in nature versus clinical in nature. Creating a Case Manager position would allow us to fulfill care management responsibilities, monitor the stability of clients in the community, and assist clients in successfully reaching their treatment goals, and\nWHEREAS, NCDMH is committed to and has demonstrated operating in a fiscally responsible manner, and\nWHEREAS, abolishing the Licensed Clinician position and creating the Case Manager position will reduce the departmental budget, is supported 100% through State Aid, and is at no cost to the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one Licensed Clinician (vacant as of November 27, 2024), Grade 11, Step 3, position number MHD300100.10817, salary $64,493.10 - 70,138.53, be abolished, effective September 28, 2025, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that one full time Case Manager, Grade 10, Step 1, position number, MHD300100.XXXXX, salary $55,705.23 \u2022 $64,675.80, be created within the Niagara County Department of Mental Health, and filled effective September 28, 2025, and be it further\nRESOLVED, the following 2025 budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE REVENUE:\nA.21.4310.000.43490.05  Reinvestment Programs  $ 4,573\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000.71010.00.10817  Positions (Licensed Clinician)  $16,803\nA.21.4310.000.78100.00  Retirement Expense  $ 2,134\nA.21.4310.000.78200.00  FICA  $ 1,285\nA.21.4310.000.78300.00  Worker's Comp  $  351\nA.21.4310.000.78400.01  Hospital/Medical Insurance  $ 4,885\nA.21.4310.000.78400.05  HRA Employer Contribution  $  354\nA.21.4310.000.78700.00  NYS Disability Insurance  $  26\nA.21.4310.000.78800.00  Flex 125  $  457\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000.71010.XXXXX  Positions (Case Manager - Mental Health)  $14,513\nA.21.4310.000.78100.00  Retirement Expense  $ 1,843\nA.21.4310.000.78200.00  FICA  $ 1,110\nA.21.4310.000.78300.00  Worker's Comp  $  303\nA.21.4310.000.78400.01  Hospital/Medical Insurance  $ 3,257\nA.21.4310.000.78400.05  HRA Employer Contribution  $  213\nA.21.4310.000. 78700.00  NYS Disability Insurance  $  26\nA.21.4310.000.78800.00  Flex 125  $  457\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CS-045-25":"Resolution No. CS-045-25\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET TRANSFER-A4310\nCOUNTY ORDERED COUNTY MANDATED COSTS\nWHEREAS, the County is required by Criminal Procedure Law Articles 730.20 and 730.50, and Family Court Act Alticle 120, to pay for Court ordered hospital in-patient stays and mental health examinations, and\nWHEREAS, as a result of the bills generated by the length of hospital stay imposed by Court Orders for year 2024, outstanding expenses currently exist, and\nWHEREAS, based on these current Court Orders the expenses to the end of the year will exceed budget, and\nWHEREAS, during the State 2021 budget process, in Spring 2020, the State determined that I 00% of the costs would be the responsibility of the Counties, negating the longstanding practice of the State paying 50% of the mandated costs, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Mental Health administers payment of these bills on behalf of the County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, should there be additional Court Ordered expenses before end of the year, another request will be necessary, and be it further\nRESOLVED, the following budget transfer be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $682,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.21.4310.000 74550.17  Department of Mental Hygiene  $682,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-095-25":"Resolution No. CSS-095-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT HIGHWAY SAFETY GRANT FOR FORENSIC LABO RA TORY\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a Highway Safety Grant for the Forensic Laboratory from the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee in the amount of $35,000 for the performance period October I, 2025 through September 30, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory is going to assist in raising people's awareness in drugged driving and reduce traffic fatalities by providing timely Toxicology services and by participating in data sharing with ITSMR, and\nWHEREAS, the Forensic Lab will use the funds to accomplish these goals by purchasing more costly supplies and equipment for the Toxicology unit's analysis in driving involved cases, and\nWHEREAS, the grant will be included in the 2026 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Highway Safety Grant for the Forensic Laboratory may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-096-25":"Resolution No. CSS-096-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT HIGHWAY TRAFFIC SAFETY GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a Highway Traffic Safety Grant from the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee in the amount of $108,071 for the performance period October 1, 2025 through September 30, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is a continuation of an existing program and is fully funded by the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used for Highway Safety education by funding position #6247 Traffic Safety Educator position, to educate pre-K and school aged children on bicycle and car safety, and to educate the public in an effort to reduce the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on the roads in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are included in the 2025 budget and will be included in the 2026 budget, now, therefore, beit\nRESOLVED, that the Traffic Safety Educator position continue to be co-terminus with the grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Highway Traffic Safety Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-097-25":"Resolution No. CSS-097-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBI2 TECHNOLOGIES SOLE SOURCE DESIGNATION FOR SUPPORT\nAND MAINTENANCE OF IRIS AND MVP SYSTEMS\nWHEREAS, the New York State Sheriffs Association made Inmate Identification and Recognition System (IRIS) and Med Verify Plus (MVP) Systems available to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office at no cost through BI2 Technologies, and\nWHEREAS, IRIS is the nation's only secure, encrypted, real-time national criminal justice data sharing network, based on iris recognition technology, which enables law enforcement agencies across the county to positively identify individuals in seconds, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office does not utilize the Med Verify Plus (MVP) module, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office wishes to continue to utilize the IRIS system which now needs annual support and maintenance, and\nWHEREAS, BI2 Technologies, LLC is the only company who can provide support and maintenance to their programs, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to waive the requirement for additional bids to purchase support and maintenance contracts for the IRIS System and that BI2 Technologies LLC be recognized as the sole source provider for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office IRIS System and its support and maintenance.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-098-25":"Resolution No. CSS-098-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT FY22 EXPLOSIVE DETECTION CANINE TEAM GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services that it is the recipient of an FY2022 Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant in the amount of $15,000 for the performance period April 1, 2025 through August 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Sheriffs Office to purchase needed law enforcement equipment including but not limited to Consultant costs to provide Explosive Detection Canine Team training, , overtime and fringe to attend training, K9 activity tracking system subscription, as well as operational and training equipment, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 budget needs to be revised in order expend the grant funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney review, the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services FY22 Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02  Civil Defense Homeland Security  $ 15,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 71050.00  Overtime Expense  $2,053\nA.17.3645.000 74500.01  Contractual Expense  $ 3,000\nA.17.3645.000 74500.02  Service Contract  $ 522\nA.17.3645.000 74600.03  Training and Education  $ 800\nA. 17.3645.000 74750.05  Law Enforcement Supplies  $7,900\nA.I 7.3645.000 78100.00  Retirement Expense  $  525\nAl 7.3645.000 78200.00  FICA Expense  $  157\nA.17.3645.000 78300.00  Workers Compensation Expense  $  43\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-099-25":"Resolution No. CSS-099-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - CREATE AND FILL TEMPORARY\nCORRECTION OFFICER POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has a Correction Officer who has been out of work with an injury since October 2024 without an anticipated return date except an anticipated 4-6 month recovery after anticipated surgery, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified of an upcoming retirement in early 2026, and\nWHEREAS, vacant positions in the Niagara County Jail create overtime and additional demands on the cun\"ent Correction Officers, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one temporary Correction Officer position be created and filled effective 9/l 7 /2025 at DSA Correction Officer Step I at an hourly rate of $26.93/hour, and, be it further\nRESOLVED, that the temporary position will be abolished upon return of the injured Correction Officer, and,\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00 Posn # 4043  Position Expense  $15,820\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3150.000 71010.00 Posn # NEW  Position Expense  $15,820\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-100-25":"Resolution No. CSS-100-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT CHILD PASSENGER SAFETY GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Traffic Safety Division has been awarded a grant from the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee in the amount of 32,000 for the performance period October 1, 2025 through September 30, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is a continuation of an existing program and is fully funded by the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, and\nWHEREAS, the goal of the grant is to increase the proper use and installation of child safety seats in New York State, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 budget needs to be amended to make part of the grant funds available in the current year,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Child Passenger Safety Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nCM.17.3989.303 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $4,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.17.3989.303 74800.16  Supplies/Service Maintenance  $4,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-101-25":"Resolution No. CSS-101-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nTELECOMMUNICATION SYSTEMS INC SOLE SOURCE DESIGNATION\nFOR SUPPORT AND MONITORING OF TEXT TO 911 SERVICE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-037-21 on April 20, 2021 approving Niagara County to enter into a contract with TeleComrnunication Systems Inc., for the installation of Text to 911 Service and 2 years of service and monitoring for a total cost of $19,150 as the lowest of three bids, and\nWHEREAS, the Text Control Center (TCC) receives the Text to 911 calls made in Niagara County from the Wireless Carriers and sends the text messages to the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Motorola Vesta 911 Call Handling equipment over an internet connection, and\nWHEREAS, Telecommunication Systems Inc., is the only company who can provide the support and maintenance for the current Text to 911 Service, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office be authorized to waive the requirement for additional quotes for service and monitoring for the TeleCommunication Systems Inc., Text Control Center and that Telecommunication Systems Inc., be recognized as the sole source provider for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Communications Center Text Control Center service and monitoring, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that after review by the County Attorney's Office, the Niagara County Sheriff be authorized to enter into a two-year agreement for support and maintenance with Telecommunication Systems Inc., pursuant to Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-102-25":"Resolution No. CSS-102-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMERGENCY SERVICE\nACCEPT BYLAWS AND APPOINT CHAIRMAN TO NIAGARA COUNTY\nLOCAL EMPERGENCY PREPAREDNESS COMMITTEE (LEPC)\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Local Emergency Preparedness Committee (LEPC) was duly established by Niagara County in accordance with the Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act (EPCRA), and\nWHEREAS, the LEPC operates under adopted bylaws, which outline the committee's structure, duties and procedures, and\nWHEREAS, the previously adopted bylaws have been reviewed and amended by two-thirds majority of the committee, and\nWHEREAS, a vacancy has occurred in the position of Chairman of the Niagara County LEPC, and\nWHEREAS, it is essential to ensure the continued effective operation and leadership of the LEPC to fulfill its responsibilities under EPCRA and protect the community from the risks of hazardous chemical releases, and\nWHEREAS, a qualified individual, Jonathan Schultz, has been identified and nominated by the LEPC to fill this critical leadership position, and\nWHEREAS, the bylaws state that any changes must be approved by the County Legislature, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County LEPC bylaws be accepted as amended, effective September 16, 2025, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Jonathan Schultz is hereby appointed as the Chairman of the Niagara County Local Emergency Preparedness Committee (LEPC).\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-103-25":"Resolution No. CSS-103-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMERGENCY SERVICES\nCREATION OF A NIAGARA COUNTY CRITICAL INCIDENT SUPPORT TEAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Emergency Services Office, Department of Mental Health, and Public Health, recognizes the need for crucial support and resources necessary to provide to first responding organizations and its members during the aftermath of critical incidents, and\nWHEREAS, Emergency Services, Mental Health, and Public Health have developed a plan to establish the Critical Incident Support Team (CIST), and\nWHEREAS, the team shall be composed of individuals with relevant expertise and will be subject to completion of the Individual and Group Crisis Intervention course by the International Critical Incident Stress Foundation, and\nWHEREAS, the CIST will provide timely and appropriate support, resources, and interventions to first responding agencies, their members, and families that have been impacted by critical incidents to mitigate the psychological and emotional strain, and\nWHEREAS, the proposed CIST plan is reasonable and necessary for the effective and responsible organization of operation of the Critical Incident Support Team (CIST) to serve Niagara County first responders, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature believes the organization and operation of a Niagara County Critical Incident Support Team (CIST) is in the best interest of the all first responding agencies and their members, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby authorizes the organization and operation of the Niagara County Critical Incident Support Team (CIST), in accordance with the proposed Plan to serve the first responders of Niagara County.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-104-25":"Resolution No. CSS-104-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMERGENCY SERVICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF ESO SOLUTIONS, INC FOR EMS\nELECTRONIC PATIENT CARE REPORTING SYSTEM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Emergency Services EMS division has an electronic patient care reporting (EPCR) system which is a requirement under New York State Public Health Law, and\nWHEREAS, the EPCR system currently used by Niagara County EMS was built out by ESO Solutions, Inc., and\nWHEREAS, ESO Solutions has provided a quote to perform said work for $13,087.10, said amount requiring two (2) quotes pursuant to Niagara County's purchasing policy, and\nWHEREAS, ESO Solutions, Inc., is the only company who can provide the support and maintenance for the EPCR system that was built for Niagara County EMS by ESO Solutions, Inc., now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby waives the purchasing policy requirement for additional quotes for support and maintenance of the EMS electronic patient care reporting system (EPCR system) as it is recognized as the sole source provider for support and maintenance for said system, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Emergency Services Office is hereby authorized to purchase the support and maintenance for the Niagara County EMS division EPCR System, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures, and said contract is authorized to be executed pursuant to Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-105-25":"Resolution No. CSS-105-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nDISTRICT ATTORNEYACCEPTANCE OF GUN INVOLVED VIOLENCE\nELIMINATION INITIATIVE GRANT\nWHEREAS, the GIVE (Gun Involved Violence Elimination) Initiative is a statewide comprehensive strategy that focuses exclusively on reducing shootings and homicides by promoting integrated, evidence based strategies, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara County Probation Department, in conjunction with the Niagara Falls Police Department, applied for the renewal of funding under the 2025-2026GIVE Initiative, and\nWHEREAS, The Niagara County District Attorney's Office has received notification that funding has been awarded in the amounts of $328,270.00 for the period of July 1, 2025 through June 30, 2026, with no required match from Niagara County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and is hereby authorized to execute the grant award documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-106-25":"Resolution No. CSS-106-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nDISTRICT ATTORNEY CRIMES AGAINST REVENUE PROGRAM GRANT ACCEPTANCE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that the Crimes Against Revenue Program grant has been renewed for the calendar year 2025, and\nWHEREAS, funding has been awarded in the amount of $68,810 for the period January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, basic revenue and appropriation figures for said program were calculated and are contained within the District Attorney's operating budget for the 2025 budget year, and\nWHEREAS, the program will continue to fund one full time Assistant District Attorney, Position #10969, ATT IV, Step 5, $120,926.00 annually, with fringe benefits which shall remain coterminous with grant funding, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this grant award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is authorized to sign the grant award documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CSS-107-25":"Resolution No. CSS-107-25\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nDISTRICT ATTORNEY MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT AND INSURANCE FRAUD\nPREVENTION PROGRAM GRANT ACCEPTANCE\nWHEREAS, The Niagara County District Attorney's Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that the Motor Vehicle Theft and Insurance Fraud Prevention Program grant has been renewed for the period of January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, funding has been awarded in the amount of $109,443 and said funds are used to offset expenses for the District Attorney's Motor Vehicle Theft and Insurance Fraud Prevention Unit, and\nWHEREAS, basic revenue and appropriation figures for said program were calculated and are contained within the CM.02.1989.114 Motor Vehicle Theft and Insurance Fraud Prevention cost center for the 2025 budget year, and\nWHEREAS, this program will continue to fund one full time Assistant District Attorney, and one full time Criminal Investigator which shall remain coterminous with grant funding, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this grant award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is authorized to execute the grant award documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CW-017-25":"Resolution No. CW-017-25\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMERGENCY SERVICES\nA WARD OF CONTRACT FOR EMERGENCY LIGHT SYSTEM REPAIR AT\nNORTH TONA WANDA RADIO COMMUNICATIONS TOWER\nWHEREAS, an unexpected light system failure occurred at the North Tonawanda Radio Communications Tower causing safety concerns for aircraft, and\nWHEREAS, the tower is approximately 300 feet tall; therefore since the U.S. FAA requires warning lights on structures greater than 200 feet to prevent collisions with aircraft, a notice to airmen (NOTAM) was submitted on September 2, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, Patriot Towers, Inc., has a history of working on the County's radio communication towers and was able to provide a quote to make the necessary repairs at a cost of$27,891, and\nWHEREAS, an Emergency Purchase Justification Form was provided to the Niagara County Purchasing Department and the Chairman of the Legislature to approve the declaration of emergency for the repair, and\nWHEREAS, the Emergency Declaration was approved on September 9, 2025 for a non-competitive award of the contract for the emergency light system repair at the North Tonawanda Radio Communications Tower to Patriot Towers, Inc., now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature accept the notice of the Emergency Light System Repair project at the North Tonawanda Radio Communications Tower, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Emergency Light System Repair project contract be awarded to Patriot Towers, Inc., and that following the County Attorney's review, the contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Elder.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CW-018-25":"Resolution No. CW-018-25\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nEMERGENCY RESTORATION WORK-BOARD OF ELECTIONS/RECORDS STORAGE BUILDING\n1961 TRANSIT RD BURT, NY 14028\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared emergency specifications for the Records Storage/Board of Elections Building located at 1961 Transit Road, Burt NY for the amount of$! 7,742.44, and\nWHEREAS, these repairs are needed as soon as possible, as the long term disrepair of the roof has resulted in numerous leaks, and\nWHEREAS, these leaks have resulted in musty, damp work areas and compromises insulation, ceiling, walls and rugs, and\nWHEREAS, the landlord selected SERVPRO, 100 Peters Rd., Bloomfield, CT 06002, to perform the required work, but has chosen to not pay the fees associated with the required repairs, and\nWHEREAS, the County has decided to pay for the repairs for the amount of $17,742.44 and deduct it from the monthly rent pursuant to the terms of Paragraph 7 of both leases, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account numbers A.10.1410.000 74200.01 and A.14.1450.000 74200.01, Rents/Leases Rent, for the amount of$17,742.44 and\nWHEREAS, the Committee of the Whole has approved the bill to be paid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE REVENUE:\nA.10.1440.000 74200.01  Rents/Leases Rent  $ 1,867.07\nA.14.1450.000 74200.01  Rents/Leases Rent  $ 15,875.37\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.15.1620.000 74800.03  Building Maint/Repairs/Supplies  $17,742.44\nRESOLVED, that the emergency repair work at Records Storage/Board of Elections Building located at 1961 Transit Road, Burt NY for the amount of$17,742.44 performed by SERVPRO, 100 Peters Rd., Bloomfield, CT 06002, be paid by the County of Niagara and deduct it from the monthly rent, now therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review and signature by the Chairman of the Legislature, that the County pay the amount of$17,742.44 to SERVPRO for the emergency repair and cleanup.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Speranza.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","CW-019-25":"Resolution No. CW-019-25\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION AUTHORIZING AND\nAPPROVING THE PURDUE DIRECT SETTLEMENT AND ENTERING\nASETTLEMENTAGREEMENT\nTO AN ACTION RELATING TO THE OPIOID CRISIS\nWHEREAS, there is a pending matter in which multiple municipalities in the State of New York have engaged in extensive negotiations for a settlement agreement (\"the Agreement\") with the following defendants: Purdue Pharma L.P., et al., regarding the companies' manufacturing and distribution of opioids, and\nWHEREAS, the proposed Settlement Agreement; with a share of monies being allocated among state and local governments across the country, which must be used in a restricted manner on \"Approved Uses\", and\nWHEREAS, political subdivisions in the State, including Niagara County will now have an opportunity to become a party to this group settlement, and\nWHEREAS, in addition to the direct settlement agreement, the settlement reached also includes a bankruptcy plan, and settlement of their ongoing bankruptcy proceedings, and\nWHEREAS, as part of the banlauptcy settlement, the County's representation Napoli Shkolnik is requesting authorization to vote on our behalf for the bankruptcy settlement, and\nWHEREAS, it is in the best interest of the County of Niagara to resolve this matter with respect to the Distributors without further litigation and execute the Settlement Agreement and Settling Subdivision Participation Release Form to participate and receive a share of the settlement and authorize Napoli Shkolnik in Bankruptcy Court, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the execution and delivery on behalf of and in the name of the County of Niagara by the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature, Rebecca J. Wydysh, or her designee, of the proposed Settlement Agreement is hereby authorized, and the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature, Rebecca J. Wydysh, or her designee, is hereby authorized, subject to the approval of the Niagara County Attorney, Claude A. Joerg, and directed to execute the proposed Settlement Agreement in a form substantially similar thereto the bankruptcy authorization to vote form, authorizing Napoli Shkolnik as designee and execute such other documents as may be necessary and appropriate to effectuate the settlement with the Distributors.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-015-25":"Resolution No. ED-015-25\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - TO COVER EMPOWER NIAGARA ALLOCATION\nREIMBURSEMENTS FOR REMAINDER OF 2025 BUDGET YEAR\nWHEREAS, Niagara County administers the Empower Niagara Program which offers low-cost hydro power allocations to qualifying small businesses, and\nWHEREAS, the low-cost hydro power allocations result in cash reimbursements to qualifying companies to assist them in paying their electric bills, and\nWHEREAS, the electric commodity rates and delivery charges in 2025 have increased at a faster rate than anticipated, totally depleting the funds made available in the 2025 budget for reimbursement to Empower Niagara businesses, and\nWHEREAS, the historically high pricing is expected to persist, with the upcoming winter having the potential to set new records for gas and electric pricing in New York State, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that in order to fulfill the County's obligations stemming from the agreements between the County and the ten existing Empower Niagara businesses, and per the advice of the County's energy consultant, that additional funds be placed in the Niagara County Economic Development 2025 budget, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2025 budget as set forth below: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.15.1620.108 42655.03  Sale of Excess Power  $80,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.15.1620.108 74500.01  Contractual  $80,000\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.813 42189.01  Activities - Eco Dev  $80,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.813 74550.30  Empower Niagara  $80,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-016-25":"Resolution No. ED-016-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION TO SET A HEARING TO CONSIDER INCLUSION OF VIABLE AGRICULTURAL\nLAND INTO NIAGARA COUNTY'S AGRICULTURAL DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, New York State Agriculture and Markets Law 25AA, Section 303-b requires a public hearing upon termination of the annual thirty (30) day period within which a land owner may submit a request for inclusion of land which is predominantly viable agricultural land within a certified agricultural district prior to the county established 8-year review period, and\nWHEREAS, a notice of the public hearing shall contain a statement that one or more requests for inclusion of predominantly viable agricultural land within a certified agricultural district have been filed with the county legislative body and shall identify the land proposed to be included, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing on the 21st day of October, at 5:45 p.m. for public and municipal input upon termination of the annual thirty (30) day period within which a land owner may submit a request for inclusion, and be it fmiher\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature shall publish the following notice in the Lockport Union Sun and Journal, and the Niagara Gazette, as well as, post such notice on the home page of the County's website, and such notice shall give in writing directly to those municipalities whose territory encompasses the lands which are proposed to be included in an agricultural district. PLEASE TAKE NOTICE that the Niagara County Legislature will conduct a public hearing on the 21st day of October at 5:45 p.m. in the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York, for the purpose of hearing public comments on the inclusion of predominantly viable agricultural land within agricultural district #7. Three (3) parcels have been proposed for inclusion. The Niagara County Agricultural and Farmland Protection Board recommends the inclusion of the following parcels of predominantly viable agricultural land into the Agricultural District: Parcel 136.00-1-49.11, consisting of 24.13 acres, located on Mapleton Road in the Town of Lockport; Parcel 136.00-1-35, consisting of 28.06 acres, located on Mapleton Road in the Town of Lockport; Parcel 108.00-1-4.1, consisting of 23.29 acres, located on Gothic Hill Road in the Town of Lockport. Questions about Agricultural District inclusion may be directed to Amanda Henning, Cornell Cooperative Extension at app27@cornell.edu.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-017-25":"Resolution No. ED-017-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAUTHORIZATION TO SUBMIT A GRANT APPLICATION TO THE\nNEW YORK STATE OFFICE OF COMMUNITY RENEWAL FOR BLOCK\nGRANT FUNDING TO REPLENISH THE MICRO-ENTERPRISE GRANT FUND\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature has requested that the Niagara County Department of Economic Development apply for funding on its behalf from the New York State Office of Community Renewal (the \"OCR\") to finance a portion of the micro-enterprise grant fund, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Development Corporation (the \"NCDC\") is assisting in the facilitation of the Program and has requested that the County provide the OCR funding to the NCDC as a grant for the purpose of assisting small businesses throughout the County, and\nWHEREAS, the Program will result in substantial benefit to Niagara County in the form of new investment and new employment positions, and\nWHEREAS, the County held a public hearing at the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 16th day of September at 5:50 p.m., to obtain citizens' views regarding the CDBG program as administered by OCR and the program, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to submit a grant application on behalf of Niagara County to the OCR to support the Program, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman is hereby designated as the Certifying Officer responsible for all activities associated with the federal environmental review process to be completed in conjunction with the Project, and\nbe it fmiher\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review the grant agreement with OCR and all related documents associated with the OCR grant shall be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contact Policy, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review the agreement with NCDC for the implementation of the Program, and administration of the OCR grant shall be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-018-25":"Resolution No. ED-018-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nFIXING DATE AND NOTICE FOR THE PUBLIC HEARING FOR THE GOLDEN WILLOW LLC\nCOMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT BLOCK GRANT APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature wishes to assess the advisability of submitting a Community Development Block Grant (\"CDBG\") application to the New York State Office of Community Renewal (the \"OCR\") for a grant to support the opening of a wedding event venue adjacent to the Sunflowers of Sanborn located at 5432 Baer Road, in Sanborn, New York, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is required to hold a public hearing to provide information to the public and to consider citizens' comments regarding community needs and the plan proposal prior to submitting an application for CDBG funding, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing whereat all interested parties shall be heard regarding the CDBG program, the County's community development and economic development programs, and the proposed funding application, and such public hearing will be held at the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, 175 Hawley Street, Lockport, New York on the 21st day of October, 2025 at 5:50 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least eight (8) days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice on the Niagara County Legislature website, and shall publish such notices once in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","ED-019-25":"Resolution No. ED-019-25\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA COUNTY ORGANIZATIONS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CULTURAL ENDOWMENT FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Cultural Endowment Funds help support numerous organizations across Niagara County that are important to local communities, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County wishes to support various cultural organizations that are committed to bringing expanded programming and events to further emich the community and citizens of all ages, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding in the amount of $320,000 for the Cultural Endowment Fund through the 2025 budget process, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the organizations listed below by appropriating $19,200 from said Cultural Endowment Fund as follows:\nNorth Tonawanda History Museum  $2,000.00\nHistorical Society of North German Settlements in Western New York  $1,700.00\nHistorical Association of Lewiston  $2,000.00\nOlcott Beach Carousel Park Association  $2,000.00\nHistorical Room at Ransomville Free Library  $2,000.00\nOakwood Cemetery Association  $1,500.00\nGreater Niagara Ballet Company, Inc.  $2,000.00\nLockport Main Street, Inc.  $2,000.00\nLewiston Council on the Arts  $2,000.00\nNiagara Academic Literacy Intervention Program, Inc.  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-134-25":"Resolution No. IF-134-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nSECURITY INFORMATION MANAGEMENT\nCAPITAL PROJECT BUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, the County applied for grant funds from the US Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Federal Funding (Administered by NY State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services (DHSES)) to enhance and sustain Niagara County's cyber security posture as well as ensure that their information systems are secure and protected from cyber incidents, and\nWHEREAS, the County has successfully secured funds from federal funding under the FY2022 Cybersecurity Grant Program, DHSES E-Grant Project ID CY22-1026-E00, in the amount of$50,000, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE\nA.16.3645.000.44305.02  Civil Defense, Homeland Security  $50,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS\nA.16.3645.000.74500.02  Contractual Expenses/Maintenance Service\nContracts  $50,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-135-25":"Resolution No. IF-135-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nBUDGET MODIFICATION FOR BUILDING MAINTENANCE AND REPAIRS\nNIAGARA COUNTY WATER DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Water District has received the amount of $73,181.00 from Niagara Mohawk Power Corporation as a rebate incentive for electrical upgrades performed at the Grand Island Pump Station, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Water District requests such funds be redirected to cover increasing costs association with building repairs at the Plant and Grand Island Pump Station, and\nWHEREAS, such a transfer will not result in any project deficit and nevertheless, there are sufficient funds in the Niagara County Water District's operating budget and fund balance to cover any project deficit if one were to occur, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, by the County Legislature of the County of Niagara, New York, that the following 2025 budget modification be effectuated: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nFX.31.8310.000 42701.01  Refund Prior Year  $73,181.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nFX.31.8330.000 74800.03  Supplies/Services, Building Maintenance  $73,181.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-136-25":"Resolution No. IF-136-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nELECTRIC VEHICLE SUPPLY EQUIPMENT LEVEL 2 CHARGING STATIONS\nFOR NIAGARA COUNTY - CHANGE ORDER NO. 2\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-091-24, dated May 14, 2024, awarded the contract for the Electric Vehicle Supply Equipment Level 2 Charging Stations, to INF Associates, LLC, 747 Third Avenue, New York, NY 10017, for a contract amount not to exceed $85,000 and amended to accommodate additional make-ready expenses for a contract total of $95,441, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $3,000 to accommodate additional trenching and materials needed at Royalton Ravine to connect the charging units to electrical panel, for a revised contract amount of $98,441, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2 to increase the contract by $3,000.00 for the installation of Level 2 charges at the Royalton Ravine Park, Krull Park and the Clyde L. Burmaster Park, for a revised contract amount of $98,441, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-137-25":"Resolution No. IF-137-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND THE NIAGARA COUNTY\nSNOWMOBILE FEDERATION OF SNOWMOBILE CLUBS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Snowmobile Federation of Snowmobile Clubs has requested the County of Niagara to agree to act as a municipal sponsor for a grant available through the New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation Snowmobile Trail Fund, and\nWHEREAS, the grant would assist the Niagara County Snowmobile Federation of Snowmobile Clubs in maintaining and operating safe recreational snowmobile trails already existing in the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, this grant would assist in grooming and coordinating trails already existing or to be added by lease or other agreements with property owners, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will build and maintain a better, safer trail system, which will in turn bring more snowmobiles here, adding dollars to the County economy, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Niagara County Snowmobile Federation of Snowmobile Clubs as appears in the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-138-25":"Resolution No. IF-138-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND SCOUTING AMERICA TROOP 58\nWHEREAS, the Scouting America Troop 58 has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use Burmaster Park on September 26th - 28'\\ 2025 for the purpose of holding a Weekend Carnpout.\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Scouting America Troop 58,and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Scouting America Troop 58 as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the agreement with Scouting America Troop 58's Weekend Campout may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County contract policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-139-25":"Resolution No. IF-139-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAWARD OF CONTRACT - COURTHOUSE MAGISTRATE COURT ROOM RENOVATIONS\nGENERAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - General Construction Project, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on August 14, 2025 as tabulated below:\n1.  The Peyton Barlow Co., Inc.  $405,000\n360 Delaware Avenue, Suite 300\nBuffalo, NY 14202\n2.  Huber Construction  $409,000\n136 Taylor Drive\nDepew, NY 14043\n3.  Javen Construction Co.  $599,000\n2575 Baird Road\nPenfield, NY 14526\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal fo1m, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nHSl0.15.1620.000 43021.00  State Aid Court Facilities Revenue  $405,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH810.15.1620.000 72200.01  Building Improvements  $405,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - General Construction Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, The Peyton Barlow Co., Inc. 360 Delaware Avenue, Suite 300, Buffalo, NY 14202, in the amount of $405,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - General Construction Project may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-140-25":"Resolution No. IF-140-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAWARD OF CONTRACT- COURTHOUSE MAGISTRATE COURT ROOM RENOVATIONS\nELECTRICAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Electrical Construction Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number HXXX.15.1620.000 72200.01, Building Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on August 14, 2025 as tabulated below:\n2.  CIR Electric  $170,000\n2517 Long Road\nGrand Island, NY 14072\n2.  Frey Electric  $179,000\n100 Pearce A venue\nTonawanda, NY 14150\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Electrical Construction Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, CIR Electric, 2517 Long Road, Grand Island, NY 14072, in the amount of$170,000, pending New York State Court approval, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Electrical Construction Project may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-141-25":"Resolution No. IF-141-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nAWARD OF CONTRACT - COURTHOUSE MAGISTRATE COURT ROOM RENOVATIONS\nMECHANICAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Mechanical Construction Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H8 l 0.15.1620.000 72200.01, Building Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on August 14, 2025 as tabulated below:\n1.  JW Danforth Company  $112,500\n300 Colvin Woods Pkwy.\nTonawanda, NY 14150\n2.  Parise Mechanical  $118,000\n1106 Sheridan Drive\nTonawanda, NY 14150\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Mechanical Construction Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, JW Danforth Company, 300 Colvin Woods Pkwy., Tonawanda, NY 14150, in the amount of $112,500, pending New York State Court approval, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Courthouse Magistrate Comt Room Renovations - Mechanical Construction Project may be executed, pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-142-25":"Resolution No. IF-142-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nA WARD OF CONTRACT- COURTHOUSE MAGISTRATE COURT ROOM RENOVATIONS\nPLUMBING CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Plmnbing Construction Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account number H8 l 0.15.1620.000 72200.01, Building Improvements, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on August 14, 2025 as tabulated below:\nI.  JW Danforth Company  $46,500\n300 Colvin Woods Pkwy.\nTonawanda, NY 14150\nand\nWHEREAS, the Infrastructure & Facilities Committee has examined the bid, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Plumbing Construction Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, JW Danforth Company, 300 Colvin Woods Pkwy., Tonawanda, NY 14150, in the amount of$46,500, pending New York State Court approval, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Courthouse Magistrate Court Room Renovations - Plumbing Construction Project may be executed, pursuant to the Niagara County Contract\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-143-25":"Resolution No. IF-143-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nCANAL ROAD REHABILITATION FROM NORTH CANAL TO NY 31\n(ROCHESTER ROAD) CONSUL TANT AW ARD\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with the Canal Road from North Canal to NY 31 (Rochester Road) Project, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in account H812.15.5 l 12.000 72600.01, Roads, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Canal Road Rehabilitation from North Canal to NY 31 (Rochester Road) Project be awarded to C & S Engineers, Inc., 499 Col Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a contract amount not to exceed $322,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IF-144-25":"Resolution No. IF-144-25\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nSHARED SERVICES AGREEMENT BETWEEN\nNIAGARA COUNTY AND THE TOWN OF CAMBRIA\nWHEREAS, the County desires to support the Town of Cambria's IT requirements by and through providing the services of the County's IT support staff, as assigned by the Director of Information Technology, in exchange for the Town of Cambria sharing the costs and expenses associated with said services provided by the County, and\nWHEREAS, it is the intent of this contract for Niagara County to provide information technology support for the Town of Cambria and to share resources that will help deliver these services in a more efficient and cost effective manner in the local communities of Niagara County through this shared service effort, and at no cost to Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the contract, the County Attorney will review the contract for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to sign the contract for shared IT services, including technologies, licensing and utilization of the same hardware and/or software products and vendors, along with the Town of Cambria, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that this contract shall be for one (1) year, with an option for four (4) one year renewals notwithstanding the provison that any party to the contract may revoke its inclusion therein by filing a notice of revocation with each signatory, effective within six (6) months of such revocation.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Myers, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-111-25":"Resolution No. IL-111-25\nFrom: Legislators Rebecca J. Wydysh, Irene M. Myers and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE HISTORICAL ASSOCIATION OF LEWISTON, INC.\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Historical Association of Lewiston, Inc. is a not-for-profit organization dedicated to preserving and promoting the rich heritage of the Lewiston area through community events and its maintenance of the Lewiston Museum, and\nWHEREAS, the association has planned its annual Lewiston Jeopardy fundraiser event for September 11, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the event will bring together association members, local leaders, and residents for a night of trivia and fun, while raising funds for the ongoing work of the Historical Association of Lewiston, Inc. and the Lewiston Museum, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support the continuation of programs and projects that enhance our local quality of life, protect our cherished historical landmarks and buildings, and promote our unique and valued heritage assets, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Historical Association of Lewiston, Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nHistorical Association of Lewiston, Inc.  $1,000.00\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-112-25":"Resolution No. IL-112-25\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON CHRISTMAS WALK WEEKEND\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the mission of The Lower Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce is to strengthen the local economy, represent the interest of business with government, promote community and build business relationships, and\nWHEREAS, the 2025 Lewiston Christmas Walk Weekend has been planned for December 5th through the 7th, 2025,and\nWHEREAS, this over 40-year old tradition highlights the Village of Lewiston merchants, the arrival of Santa Claus to the Village, a weekend of family events and activities, culminating in the Lewiston Christmas Lights Parade, and\nWHEREAS, the event will draw large crowds of visitors to the region from surrounding Western New York communities and Canada, to the benefit oflocal businesses, and\nWHEREAS, an event of this magnitude can only be successful through full community participation and sponsorships, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lewiston Christmas Walk Weekend by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nThe Lower Niagara River Region Chamber of Commerce  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-113-25":"Resolution No. IL-113-25\nFrom: Legislator Carla L. Speranza and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF WRIGHTS CORNERS FIRE COMPANY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wrights Corners Fire Company, a 50l(c)(3), is a 100% volunteer fire company, and was established in 1939, and\nWHEREAS, the Wrights Corners Fire Company provides Fire/Rescue and EMS services through its main hall at 4043 Lake Avenue in the Town of Lockport and its two substations at 5515 Shunpike Road and 7103 Rochester Road in the Town of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, volunteer fire companies across the region are struggling to attract and retain qualified volunteers and meeting increasing training and safety requirements, and\nWHEREAS, we believe that the Wrights Corners Fire Company benefits not only persons in the Town of Lockport, but also in neighboring communities such as Newfane and Hartland and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorizes the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Wrights Corners Fire Company by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nWrights Corners Fire Company  $834.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-115-25":"Resolution No. IL-115-25\nFrom: Legislator Carla L. Speranza and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE DALE ASSOCIATION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Dale Association is a 501 ( c) (3) charitable organization, and as a human services agency dedicated to enriching the lives of individuals and providing supportive programs and services to the community, and\nWHEREAS, the Dale Association was founded in 1951 as a senior citizens club in response to older people living alone and in need of social and recreational opportunities, and\nWHEREAS, the Dale Association faces significant cuts in funding, and\nWHEREAS, adult residents in the Town and City of Lockport may receive services for themselves or as caregivers to those in need through the Dale Association, and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorizes the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of The Dale Association by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nThe Dale Association  $5,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-116-25":"Resolution No. IL-116-25\nFrom: Legislator Carla L. Speranza and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA COUNTY FEDERATION OF CONSERVATION CLUBS\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Federation of Conservation Clubs (NCFCC) is a 501c3 organization encompassing most of the active fishing, hunting, trapping clubs and organizations in Niagara County, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Federation of Conservation Clubs advises member clubs of the latest fishing, hunting and environmental legislation and pending legislation through its administrative address 702 Ridge Road, Lewiston, NY 14092, and\nWHEREAS, the NCFCC is also active in recommending to the New York State Conservation Council, ideas spawned by our member clubs pertaining to environmental laws such as bag limits, size limits, seasons, and other issues, and\nWHEREAS, NCFCC sponsors and provides education, training and sporting events throughout the year across Niagara County for people of all ages and promotes awareness of wildlife and habitat conservation, and\nWHEREAS, we believe that the Niagara County Federation of Conservation Clubs benefits not only residents of Niagara County, but also visitors who choose to participate in hunting and fishing sports throughout the year, and the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support this, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorizes the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Niagara County Federation of Conservation Clubs by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara County Federation of Conservation Clubs  $500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-117-25":"Resolution No. IL-117-25\nFrom: Legislator Jeffrey Elder and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LASALLE-GRIFFON VETERANS FOR FOREIGN WARS\nPOST 917 THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the VFW Foundation is a 501 (c) (3) and is the official charity of the VFW, the oldest and largest organization of combat veterans in the United States, and\nWHEREAS, the LaSalle-Griffon Veterans of Foreign Wars (VFW) Post 917 mission is to foster camaraderie among United States veterans of overseas conflicts, to serve our veterans, the military and our communities, and to advocate on behalf of all veterans, and\nWHEREAS, the LaSalle-Griffon VFW Post 917 ensures that veterans are respected for their service, always receive their earned entitlements, and are recognized for the sacrifices they and their loved ones have made on behalf of this great country, and\nWHEREAS, the LaSalle-Griffon VFW Post 917 is a strong organization working to ensure that Veterans of Foreign Wars have a place to call their own in Niagara Falls, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of LaSalle-Griffon Veterans of Foreign Wars (VFW) Post 917 by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nLaSalle-Griffon Veterans of Foreign Wars (VFW) Post 917  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-118-25":"Resolution No. IL-118-25\nFrom: Legislator Jeffrey Elder and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA PRIDE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Niagara Pride is a 50l(c) (3) nonprofit organization serving LGBTQIA + individuals and families in Niagara Falls, and surrounding communities, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Pride offers services and programs to help ensure of diversity programs in schools, and\nWHEREAS, through community support, Niagara Pride can continue to provide trainings for businesses, schools and agencies to build a better life for themselves and their community, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Pride works to provide Pride Celebrations and much more throughout Niagara County,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Niagara Pride by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nNiagara Pride  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-119-25":"Resolution No. IL-119-25\nFrom: Legislators Christopher A. Robins, Jeffrey Elder, Christopher Voccio, Christopher J. McKimmie and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA FALLS VETERANS MONUMENT COMMISSION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Niagara Falls Veterans Monument Commission is an important organization that runs a world class veterans' monument in Hyde Park in Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Falls Veterans Monument Commission is planning several improvements to the monument park, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Niagara Falls Veterans Monument Commission by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara Falls Veterans Monument Commission  $3,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-120-25":"Resolution No. IL-120-25\nFrom:  Legislators Christopher A. Robins, Randy R. Bradt, Jesse P. Gooch and Economic Development\nCommittee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NIAGARA WHEATFIELD ATHLETICS ALUMNI\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature recognizes the importance of providing athletic and recreational opportunities for the youth in our community, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara Wheatfield Athletics Alumni is the parent organization for Niagara Wheatfield Amateur Athletics (NW AA) Football, a youth football program in the Town of Niagara and Town of Wheatfield area, and\nWHEREAS, NWAA Football takes the safety of its youth athletes very seriously and has identified a need for additional funding to replace and recertify football helmets and shoulder pads for their youth athletes, and\nWHEREAS, without assistance, the rising costs of equipment would contribute to higher registration fees to participate, which may be burdensome on families and prevent children from participating, and now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Niagara Wheatfield Athletics Alumni by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nNiagara Wheatfield Athletics Alumni  $3,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-121-25":"Resolution No. IL-121-25\nFrom: Legislators Christopher A. Robins, Christopher Voccio and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION OF SUPPORT FOR THE AQUARIUM OF NIAGARA\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Aquarium of Niagara is an anchor of downtown Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the mission of the Aquarium of Niagara is, with our community and partners, we celebrate our natural wonders and inspire people to make a difference for aquatic life, and\nWHEREAS, the Aquarium of Niagara continues to expand, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorizes the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Aquarium of Niagara by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nAquarium of Niagara  $1,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-122-25":"Resolution No. IL-122-25\nFrom: Legislator Christopher J. McKimmie and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF CREATE A HEALTHIER NIAGARA FALLS COLLABORATIVE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Create a Healthier Niagara Falls Collaborative has long been committed to improving the health, wellness, and safety of residents throughout the City of Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Niagara Falls has experienced an increased number of incidents involving bicycles, E-Bikes, and E-Scooters, many resulting in serious injuries and in some cases fatalities, and\nWHEREAS, the use of properly fitted bicycle helmets is a proven and effective means of preventing head injuries and saving lives, particularly among children, and\nWHEREAS, the Create a Healthier Niagara Falls Collaborative intends to purchase and distribute bicycle helmets for children in the LaSalle area of Niagara Falls as part of its ongoing public safety and health initiatives, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Create a Healthier Niagara Falls Collaborative by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund for the purpose of purchasing and distributing bicycle helmets for children in the City of Niagara Falls, as follows:\nCreate a Healthier Niagara Falls Collaborative  $1,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-123-25":"Resolution No. IL-123-25\nFrom: Legislators Christopher J. McKimmie, Christopher A. Robins, Jeffrey Elder and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE AMERICAN LEGION LASALLE POST 1142\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the American Legion LaSalle Post 1142, is located in the heart of the LaSalle business district along Buffalo Avenue in the City of Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, the American Legion LaSalle Post 1142 is home to more than 123 military Veterans, 40 Sons of the American Legion, and 50 Auxiliary members, and\nWHEREAS, the American Legion LaSalle Post 1142 plays a vital role in supporting and advocating for the needs of our military veterans, and\nWHEREAS, the American Legion is committed to maintaining the LaSalle property as a destination and resource for not only military veterans but members of the community at large, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the American Legion LaSalle Post 1142 by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nAmerican Legion LaSalle Post 1142  $4,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-124-25":"Resolution No. IL-124-25\nFrom:  Legislators Christopher J. McKimmie, Christopher A. Robins, Christopher Voccio and Economic\nDevelopment Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE FRIENDS OF THE NIAGARA FALLS PUBLIC LIBRARY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature recognizes the importance of providing educational and recreational opportunities for the youth in our community, and\nWHEREAS, the Friends of the Niagara Falls Public Library is a volunteer organization that supports library programs, materials, and services through fundraising and advocacy, and\nWHEREAS, the Friends of the Library have identified a need for additional funding to enhance and expand youth programming at the LaSalle Branch of the Niagara Falls Public Library, and\nWHEREAS, these programs serve to inspire and engage local youth, promoting literacy, creativity, and lifelong learning, and now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Friends of the Niagara Falls Public Library by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nFriends of the Niagara Falls Public Library  $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-125-25":"Resolution No. IL-125-25\nFrom: Legislator Randy R. Bradt and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 16, 2025\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE NORTH TONAWANDA ATHLETIC ASSOCIATION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the North Tonawanda Athletic Association (NTAA) is a 501 (c)3 organization that offers a variety of athletic programs to children in the City of North Tonawanda, and\nWHEREAS, NT AA strives to provide football and cheerleading programs that focuses on personal growth, team development, leadership skills and participant safety, and\nWHEREAS, equipment upgrades are needed to ensure that the athletes are able to safely participate in games, competitions and practices, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports North Tonawanda Athletic Association appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nNorth Tonawanda Athletic Association  $7,700.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent.","IL-126-25":"Resolution No. IL-126-25\nFrom:  Legislators Irene M. Myers, Christopher J. McKimmie, Irene M. Myers, Christopher J. McKimmie,\nRebecca J. Wydysh, Randy R. Bradt, David E. Godfrey, Richard L. Andres, Jesse P. Gooch, Anthony J. Nemi, Richard E. Abbott, Michael A. Hill, Shawn A. Foti, Christopher Voccio, Christopher A. Robins, Jeffrey Elder and Carla L. Speranza\nDated: September 16, 2025\nA RESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF CONGRESSMAN NICK LANGWORTHY'S ENERGY CHOICE\nACT (H.R. 3699, S. 1945) AND IN OPPOSITION TO GOVERNMENT\nMANDATED NATURAL GAS BANS\nWHEREAS, affordable and reliable energy is essential to the health, safety, and economic prosperity of New York families, businesses, and communities, and\nWHEREAS, natural gas remains a dependable, cost-effective, and clean-burning energy source used by millions of New Yorkers to heat their homes, cook their food, and power their businesses, and\nWHEREAS, in 2019, New York State enacted the Climate Leadership and Community Protection Act (CLCP A), which mandates aggressive emissions reductions and serves as the foundation for sweeping energy restrictions, including efforts to phase out natural gas, and\nWHEREAS, building on the CLCPA, Governor Kathy Hochul and the New York State Legislature in 2023 enacted provisions in the state budget banning natural gas and other fossil fuel hookups in most new residential and commercial buildings, effective 2026 for smaller buildings, and 2029 for larger buildings, thereby eliminating consumer choice and limiting access to affordable energy, and\nWHEREAS, Governor Hochul has publicly supported these bans and related measures as part of her administration's climate and energy agenda, despite widespread concerns that such mandates will increase costs, strain grid reliability, and restrict energy diversity, and\nWHEREAS, these top-down policies undermine affordability, threaten reliability during peak demand, and strip away the freedom of consumers and businesses to choose the energy sources that best meet their needs, and\nWHEREAS, Congressman Nick Langworthy of New York and Senator Jim Justice of West Virginia introduced the Energy Choice Act (H.R. 3699, S. 1945), federal legislation that ensures state and local governments cannot restrict consumer access to natural gas and other affordable energy sources, thereby protecting freedom of choice for New Yorkers and all Americans, and\nWHEREAS, the Energy Choice Act would safeguard households, small businesses, hospitals, farmers, and manufacturers from harmful government overreach and preserve access to an \"all-of-the-above\" energy strategy that strengthens reliability and affordability, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature strongly supports the Energy Choice Act as introduced by Congressman Langw01thy (H.R. 3699) and Senator Justice (S. 1945) and stands firmly opposed to New York State's natural gas bans, the CLCPA-driven restrictions, and other state energy mandates that eliminate consumer choice, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature urges Congress to pass the Energy Choice Act to defend consumer choice, protect energy affordability, and preserve reliable access to natural gas for New Yorkers, and\nRESOLVED, that copies of this resolution be sent to Congressman Nick Langworthy, Senator Jim Justice, the New York Congressional delegation, Senators Chuck Schumer and Kirsten Gillibrand, Governor Kathy Hochul, and leadership of the New York State Legislature.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Myers, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pe1tains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by McKimmie, seconded by Myers.\nAdopted. 15 Ayes, 0 Noes, 0 Absent."};
